Hi again,

I think I tracked my problem down.  I read the alignment section in the
document located at:

ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/DEC/DECinfo/document/EC-N0736-43.ps.Z

and I found out that alignment on Alpha chips is either on 4 byte or 8
byte boundaries.

It turns out that the line:

Target_l = (unsigned long *)
  (string_glyph.bits + ((v_anchor + j) * paddedW / 8) + ByteOffset);

made Target_l aligned to a four byte boundary (I suppose that this may
be because the expression being cast is aligned to a four byte boundary.
Or am I full of it?).

So of course, when accessing a pointer to an unsigned long (which I know
has to aligned to a eight byte boundary), we get the unaligned access
error.

My question is now this:  is there a way of forcing data to be aligned
to eight byte boundaries?  For instance, with a #pragma statement, or a
compiler option?

Thanks,

Tom

