elsen@imec.be (Marc Elsen) writes:

> Recently we upgraded our hpux file servers (10.20) to 
> very recent patch releases which amongst fixing
> many problems (ahem) also implements NFS V3 in hpux.

> We have some osf / digital unix /tru 64 Unix clients
> (what's in a name ...) running 4.0d
> which always give the following error when listing
> a directory which is mounted on the hpux file server :

> % ls -lt | more
>: Error 10005 occurred.
>-rw-r--r--   1 user    group         621 Jun 24 10:06 some.file

Is this directory listing complete?

>  NFS3ERR_TOOSMALL -> The buffer or request is too small.


> Support centers are in these complex ages of course 
> pointing to each other yet the question remains
> interesting  a to whether the nfs client (digital unix)
> or the hpux server has a bug or problem ?

Gee, I thought I had trained ours to ask for tcpdump traces before
pointing fingers.  :-(

My guess is that Tru64 sends a READDIRPLUS request, HP allocates buffers,
but overflows and returns the error instead of the partial buffer.

You might be able to reproduce the problem with a directory with lots
of short names (or would that be a lot of long names?).  In general,
we'll issue READDIRPLUS for the first access of a directory, and then
READDIR for subsequent reads, relying on the name cache for holding
file attributes.  Therefore, you may see an error on the first "ls",
but see it work on subsequent runs.

In the five years we've been shipping NFS V3 I can't recall hearing
of this problem, however, that doesn't make me completely confident
that it's solely HP's bug.  (Not that I'd ever point a finger!)

At any rate, tcpdump, tcpdump, tcpdump!  See recent postings here
about setup and usage hints from the Digital Unix/Tru64 FAQ.

	-Ric Werme
