Tobias Ernst wrote:

> seeing that you really seem to care about removing Dec C peculiarities, there
> is another one which has bothered me numerous times:
> 
>   #include <stdio.h>
>   int main(void)
>   {
>       printf ("\x1B[2JThe screen is cleared.\n");
>       return 0;
>   }

In the program above, the difference is that \x escape sequences are
not recognised in "common" (or "K&R") mode. If compiled in one of the
ANSI modes: -std (Relaxed ANSI) or -std1 (Strict ANSI), you will see
the "expected" result using DEC C (now Compaq C).

> Gnu C can recognise "\x1B" as a way of encoding the character with
> charactercode 0x1B (27, the escape character, here used to produce an
> ANSI/VT100 sequence to clear the screen) into the string. Numerous other
> compilers (esp. in the Dos/Windows world) also do. Dec C won�t, ...

Yes, the difference here is that DEC C in UNIX V4.0x defaults to
-std0 ("common" or "K&R") mode. Compilers in UNIX V5.0 and later,
as well as our Developers' Toolkit compiler will default to -std.
As such, you would get the "expected" results by default using
any of the newer compilers.

You can change your default locally by changing the "comp.config"
file in /usr/lib/cmplrs/cc. This is described in the cc(1) man page.

> and the problem is that Dec C does not even give a warning, so programs
> that use it may fail very subtly at run time.

You can enable a message for this by adding -w0, which tells the
compiler to turn on informational messages. See below:

% cc -std0 t.c -w0    
cc: Info: t.c, line 6: An undefined escape sequence was encountered; the
backslash is being ignored. (undefescap)
    printf ("\x1B[2JThe screen is cleared.\n");
--------------^

I hope this helps answer your questions.
Feel free to let us know about other "peculiarities"!

-Jeff
