In article <uijrKC0u#GA.359@nih2naac.compuserve.com>, "Gerhard Stegemann" <GStegemann@csi.com> writes:
>
>I'm running a AlphaStation 200 under OSF/1 3.2A.

>Is it really necessary to start from scratch (creating a new file system)
>or are there alternatives to migrate some components from 3.2A,

If you have all the necessary intermediate versions you can upgrade 
without having to start from scratch.  The sequence is (as documented
in the installation guide):

V3.2A -> V3.2C -> V4.0A -> V4.0B -> V4.0D or V4.0E -> V4.0F

>like password files, configuration files, etc.?

If you're not using C2 security, the password and group file are
the same format.  Over a wide range of versions some of the reserved
user or group IDs may change.  Similarly, startup and configuration
files may change format or content slightly.  Blindly replacing a
new file with an old one is likely to cause problems.  For a lot
of things, it might be simplest to just run the setup scripts and
let them handle any differences.

>
