    Windows NT

    User's manual

He had Linux running on it, but now he wants to run Digital Unix =
(Tru64?) on
it, because he needs an application that is not available for Linux.

When this PC is powered up, it runs the AlphaBIOS 5.62-1. I assume that =
this=20
is the ARC version of the console version. For Digital Unix one needs =
SRM.
(I have no experience with ARC. We have experience with other Alpha =
systems
running OpenVMS and Digital Unix using SRM.)

In the (advanced) CMOS setup menu, there is the possibility to switch to =
SRM=20
(one for OpenVMS and one for Digital Unix), but none of them work. If =
SRM=20
is selected, the PC still comes up with the AlphaBIOS after power up,=20
although now the SRM is shown as selected in the CMOS menu.

I tried to upgrade to a newer firmware version. I downloaded =
fwupdate.exe
from=20
http://ftp.europe.digital.com/pub/DEC/Alpha/firmware/readmes/v5.4/alphapc=
164sx/
and placed it on a blank FAT formatted 3.5" diskette. According to the=20
instructions on this web site, I tried the update AlphaBIOS menu option =
in=20
the BIOS setup. The system starts to read the diskette for some seconds =
and=20
then it hangs (no activity seen for several minutes).
I also tried to power on the PC with this diskette inserted and the =
fail-safe
switch in the position for a fail-safe boot. After some beeps, the =
system
starts to read the diskette. It than seems to start with SRM, as I see =
the
self-test count-down, familiar from the other Alpha systems.
It then complains that it cannot find the "option firmware files" and =
then=20
it enters the firmware update utility that I know from the other Alpha =
systems.=20
The UPDATE command indicaties that "fsb" is loaded and verified, but it=20
complains about "fw" (open failure firmware filename 'nt_fw'). After =
power up,=20
again AlphaBIOS 5.62-1 comes up.

I now start to doubt whether this system is able to run SRM.
Does anyone has an idea how to make SRM running on this machine?

                                                F.Z.




