In article <fMVb3.81$U16.468@news.megsinet.net>, Albert Chin-A-Young <china@the--written--word.com> wrote:
> I was just quoted $1800 for DEC C++. Sun C/C++ is $1000. HP-UX C++ is
> $1300. IRIX C++ is $600.
> 
> Why is DEC charging so much?
> 

Have you ever used the Sun or HP compilers? Their compiler technology
blows chunks...

Sounds like you were quoted a 'Traditional-Use License', which licenses
anyone on the system to use the C++ compiler. Please note that there 
are also Concurrent-Use and Personal-Use licenses, which license
individual users that go for about 1/5th the cost of the Traditional License.
Concurrent-Use is first-come, first-served; while Personal-Use is bound
to a username. The breakeven point is about 5 licenses; i.e., if you have
more than 5 users that need simultaneous access to the compiler, then
the Traditional Use license is the better purchase.

Craig

