"Frank J. Perricone" <frank@dlc.state.vt.us> writes:

> I don't anticipate problems with the roll-forward, except possibly that some
> programs might object about licenses that have expired and refuse to run.
> I'm less sure about the rollback.  I'll be working in a test copy of our
> database, of course, but what effects can I expect on the file system?  What
> steps do I have to take beforehand (other than the inevitable pair of full
> backups that I hope to not have to use) to prepare?  What will I need to do
> to recover?  What are the gotchas?

an area in which we had a lot of unexpected problems was this: during
the course of normal operations during the roll-forward, a lot of files
get "touched" to the new date, and remain that way after the
roll-back.  user mailboxes, caches, application logs, and all manner of
other files have their timestamps set to the forward date, and this
causes some problems when the date is set back to normal.

in particular, our sysadmin insisted that we go back and manually touch
all the files back to the real time -- he said that the restore from
backup might not run correctly if there were forward-dated files.  and
not just last-modified dates, either.  there are _three_ timestamps for
each file: last-modified, last-accesed, and last-mode-change.

