njmp_walti@njmp_solid.phys.ethz.ch (Christoph Walti) writes:

> Hi,
> 
> recently, I compared the speed of a very simple program (posted below),
> which was once compiled using DecC and a second time using GNU gcc-2.8.1
> on a Digital-alpha staion DS20 with DUnix4.0F. To my very surprise, the
> DecC-compiled version was at least 30, if not even 50 times as fast as the
> gcc-compiled version! I did not use any optimisation flags, but the ones
> which are set by default (are there any? I don't know.)
> Does someone know, where this enormous difference comes from? Does the
> DecC compiler realize, that in this loop nothing is to do anyway, and
> therefore, the result is known from the very beginning and can be put in
> the executable directly?
> 
> Thanks for your help
> 
> Chris
> 

On DU4.0F, the DEC C compiler defaults to -O1 (minimal optimization).
If memory serves, gcc defaults to -O2.

I've seen postings where user testing has shown that the DEC C math
libraries can improve performance of calculation-intensive code by a
factor of 50 or more.  This is because they were crafted for the Alpha
CPU, so can best take advantage of it. Gcc has no/darn little Alpha-specific
optimization capabilities. Egcs is better, but not much. If you want speed,
use the DEC C compiler.

> 
> PS1: This difference in speed persists, when the printf-statement is
> removed.

Again, math libraries make the difference.

> PS2: The gcc version was compiled on this machine (and should be
> optimized for ev6).
> 

Compiling natively will only make gcc run faster on the DS20. It will not
allow gcc to produce better EV6-specific binaries.

MarkG
