The main reason I'm marginally trying to avoid that approach is because it's
scary.  I've done restores of selected files from tapes to verify that the
backup works, but I've never wiped my system and restored it from the ground
up, and I'm concerned that something will go wrong -- either because of tape
problems, or because of me simply not being experienced enough in such
things, having never done it in Unix.  So I was hoping to use an approach
where the full-restore process was used as a last resort in case the primary
method of roll-back recovery wasn't working.

(Also, this needs to be done by the evening of the Saturday I do it.  We
have stuff that runs at night every night and we can't skip a night.)

