Hello everyone.  This is probably a basic question, but I have not
found the solution yet so I thought I would float it.  I have a host
running OSF 4.0d.  After everyone leaves for the day I am going to
have cron start a program "foo".  Foo should run until midnight, when
it needs to be killed so that out nightly backups can happen.

Problem is that: a) foo has no -kill or similar tag; b) foo does not
drop the process number into a pid file that can be piped into a kill
command.

So my question, is ther a simple way to set up a shell script that
cron can call at midnight that  will grep "foo" out of the ps command,
and then kill -PID for foo?  I am not much of a shell script
programmer, and only play a system administrator on TV, so those are
two strikes against me.  Any help or pointers to the right resources
would be much appreciated.

--chris

