Mark Lambert <markl@fulcrum.com.au> writes:
>Serguei Patchkovskii had a more elegant awk solution, but mine is (I think)
>more understandable for non-awk guru's...
>the brute force approach :0
>try
>kill `ps -ef | grep foo | grep -v grep | awk '{print $2}'`

Danger!  Will Robinson!  Danger!   :-)

Is this cron job to run as root?   What if I'm running an important job?

	/local/sbin/food -for the-hungry

Will they not starve?

One should ensure not "foo", but the complete path (e.g. "/local/sbin/foo")
appears in the process table.  Then one should test for equality, using
something like the awk solution provided by Serguei.  You could also use

	0 0 * * * kill `/sbin/init.d/bin/getpid /local/sbin/foo`

The getpid script is used by the init.d (rc2.d and rc3.d) scripts when
the relevant daemons aren't using /var/run.  It provides a variant of
Serguei's solution.

One still might worry about multiple instances...

Perhaps it would be better to wrap your program with a script like:

	#!/bin/sh
	echo $$ > /var/run/foo.pid
	exec /local/sbin/foo

and use 

	0 0 * * * kill `cat /var/run/foo.pid`

though this will fail if foo forks a new process and exits.

Paul Fardy
